David Ben-­Gurion, Israel’s first prime minister, had the initiative and vision to gather in Jerusalem as many Hebrew manuscripts as possible. His goal was to create a comprehensive collection that would be used for academic research and religious and cultural enrichment. Once it became clear that it would be impossible to physically collect all these manuscripts, Ben-­Gurion decided to establish an institute where photographs of them would be stored, and in 1950, the Institute of Microfilmed Hebrew Manuscripts was established. Today, the institute is an integral part of the National Library and has managed to collect microfilm images of approximately 95 percent of all the Hebrew manuscripts identified in the world. The catalog of Hebrew manuscripts in the NLI, with its seventy thousand to eighty thousand volumes that range from ancient manuscripts dating to before the tenth century to modern manuscripts from the twentieth century, represents the largest and most important surviving “virtual collection” of Hebrew manuscript metadata.2
