knowledge in an electronic form for future generations. But scanning alone is not enough to make these documents accessible in their entirety, since it provides only an image of the scanned page. It does not allow for searching, copying, and analyzing the text found in the scanned document. For these purposes, the scan must be converted from an image format to machine-­ readable form.
This process of turning an image into a machine-­readable form has been already available since the mid-­1960s and has made considerable progress in recent decades.5 The process usually involves three steps: (1) layout analysis (identifying text regions, finding lines of text, attributing semantic tags to the lines and the regions), (2) simultaneous character recognition and word recognition, and sometimes (3) post-­processing with language models adapted to ensure the reliability and quality of the prediction.6
The success of the automatic text recognition (ATR) depends on many factors, including the quality of the scan, the quality of the printing, the type of text, the type of font, and the language of the text. The precision of the ATR is measured by the character error rate (CER) and the word error rate (WER) in the text. The fewer errors there are in the translation of the image into text, the higher the quality and accuracy of the resulting text.
A distinction must be made between the processes for handwritten texts and printed texts. The process for the former is referred to as handwritten text recognition (HTR); the process for the latter is called optical text recognition (OCR). Today, OCR systems are capable of recognizing text with a very high degree of accuracy.7 HTR software is still under development, and it faces additional challenges compared to OCR: the uniqueness
