HTR and OCR in Hebrew Letters
To produce accurate transcriptions of printed text in English, various computer software options have been available for many years. These tools, such as the free open-­source software Tesseract and Google Docs, as well as the commercial software ABBYY, have proven to be effective in handling this type of text. However, even ABBYY, which can handle printed modern Hebrew text, lacks the necessary sophistication to accurately transcribe historical materials, particularly handwritten manuscripts.
The Hebrew language presents unique challenges due to several distinct characteristics that set it apart from Latin-­based languages. They include the use of different characters, right-­to-­left writing direction as opposed to left-­to-­right, and a distinctive morphological and orthographical structure.9 Hebrew exhibits a richer morphology compared to English, with linguistic estimates indicating approximately seventy million valid (inflected) forms in Hebrew, whereas English has only one million.10 In rabbinic literature, there are approximately seventeen thousand different abbreviations, 35 percent of which are ambiguous.11 These challenges are further amplified when dealing with manuscripts.
