One of the prominent approaches in text processing in recent years is natural language analysis and machine learning techniques, such as neural networks. A deep learning approach is based on neural networks that learn different conversion patterns between input and output. The disadvantage of the neural network is the huge amount of manually labeled data required for training, which are often not available. This is also the reason why, until recently, these methods were not used to convert text in Hebrew letters, as there are not sufficient target data to train neural networks in text in Hebrew letters.12 The exponential increase in digitized materials has led to many efforts to create accessible texts in a machine-­readable form; in fact, several large projects have achieved success in this area.
In the past decade, several projects have been developed with the aim of building a web-­based infrastructure capable of successfully converting historical manuscripts with different writing systems. This article specifically concentrates on Transkribus.13
The Transkribus Platform
Transkribus is a comprehensive platform for the digitization, artificial intelligence-­powered text recognition, transcription, and search of historical documents from any place, any time, and in any language (https://readcoop. eu/transkribus). Transkribus was launched in 2015 with funding from the European Commission’s Seventh Framework Programme (FP7), as part of the tranScriptorium Project (2013–15; https://eadh.org/projects/ transcriptorium).14 Transkribus enables the downloading of documents and
